## Identity, not index if you can help it

A list item needs a stable identity so React can tell which row stayed and which row moved. Pretend each item is `{ key, label }`. The key is the id you already know; the label is what you show. Prefer that key over the array index when items can reorder, because an index is only a slot number and that slot number changes when the list changes.

This bites when two rows swap and you keyed them `0` and `1`. React thinks row 0 is still the same component and can leave stale input or focus on the wrong line. A stable key keeps the identity attached to the item, not to the seat it happened to sit in.

```javascript
const data = [
  { key: 1, label: "a" },
  { key: 2, label: "b" }
];
return data.map((item) => item.key + ":" + item.label).join(",");
```

A common mistake is mapping the raw strings and never mentioning `key`. The exercise asks you to return `1:a,2:b` from items that carry a key and a label, and to mention `key` in the source so the habit sticks.
